ld be in  docker containers they could be on the  same physical system that you're on it  could be on a different system so  there's different spawners with  different parameters and putting it all  together  you've kind of got the conceptual view  on the right-hand side and mins more  block diagram on the left-hand side so  in this case when we've got a group of 6  users we're gonna have six individual  notebook servers and each one will have  their own and each one will have a URL  that they access their notebook server  with so when would we want to use  Jupiter hub well it could be used in a  class where you have students that are  doing homework and you might combine it  with something like env grader which  does some automated grading of student  assignments so basically you want to  ensure that the students have a baseline  system with which to work and they're  all working with the same dependencies  and things and you don't want to  necessarily have them install every  single package if they're perhaps not  technically savvy so things like you  might be teaching a GIS class but it's  more to geography students who aren't  computer scientists to have them Khanda  install or pip install gets to be kind  of burdensome for the TAS and the  professors so Jupiter hub is a good  alternative there if you're doing a  short-lived workshop an installation is  particularly annoying or hard  maybe you have to build different  dependencies and C or something you  could use psychic build which would be  an excellent option and you should check  it out and but it simplifies having to  have every person running when you're  running a workshop have to build their  own configuration and you wind up with  somebody's laptop that's like ancient  and somebody else's whose is brand-new  and running beta version of whatever it  simplifies that sometimes you just  simply have a research group and a small  cluster and you just want to be able to  share notebooks files database resources  whatever with that small group and that  makes sense or if you want to use some  on-site computing resources within an  institution that you want to give access  to and you know in a sort of sort of a  streamlined way when would we not want  to use Jupiter hub remember Jupiter hub  is authenticated and persistent so if we  want to just spin up a notebook and we  don't care if it goes away after we're  done with the session then maybe Jupiter  hubs not what you need maybe it's  something like temp n B that you can  create anonymous notebooks and they go  away binder is another project it's not  one that project Jupiter manages but we  do have people that contribute to it  binder is actually temp MB that you can  take a github repo upload it and then  people can use the notebook  interactively and it's really a nice  thing and Jeremy Freeman and the whole  group that works on finder has done a  great job and then sage math cloud is a  good option if you want a hosted service  that also enables real-time  collaboration over  individual notebook as far as where  would you find more resources if you  wanted to try out Jupiter hub we have a  couple of reference deployments one is  using docker and I think Peter probably  could answer some of the questions on it  and another one is using no docker  because what we found was some of the  professors that were setting it up for  their class didn't feel as comfortable  with docker or couldn't use it in their  environment so it's a you can deploy it  with a bunch of ansible strip scripts on  a bare metal server again mins pilot  data London talk is fabulous and I  highly encourage you to take a look at  it we've also got a repo associated with  it so there is configuration scripts and  things that let you configure it in  several different ways that coordinate  with the tutorial and in July we did a  mini workshop it was sort of HPC  oriented so high performance computing  oriented but we had different members of  our comm